# linkchecker

A colorful terminal tool that crawls a website and finds broken links, missing images, and links that use a bare IP address. It saves a checklist you can work through in Bear or any Markdown app.

![linkchecker crawling a site: counters for pages, links, OK, broken, and blocked; a progress bar; the requests in flight; and a list of broken links with the pages they appear on](linkchecker.png)

It is one Python file. Run it with [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/) and there is nothing to install.

```zsh
uv run linkchecker.py https://example.com
```

## What it does

- **Crawls the whole site.** It starts from the home page and the sitemap (`sitemap.xml`, `sitemap_index.xml`, `wp-sitemap.xml`), then follows every link on the site.
- **Checks every link it finds,** on your site and on other sites.
- **Finds missing images everywhere they hide:** `<img src>`, every size in `srcset`, lazy-load attributes (`data-src`, `data-lazy-src`, `data-srcset`), `og:image` and `twitter:image` tags, and links that point at image files.
- **Flags URLs that use an IP address** (for example `http://203.0.113.7/wp-content/...`) and gives you the fixed URL. These links break when the server moves, and they fail HTTPS checks.
- **Recommends search-and-replace fixes, and tests them first.** See [Recommendations](#recommendations).
- **Keeps "blocked" apart from "broken."** Sites like LinkedIn and Amazon refuse automated requests with 401, 403, 429, or 999. Those links usually work in a browser, so the tool lists them for a check by hand.
- **Spots WordPress maintenance mode** before it starts, so you don't get a report full of false errors.
- **Is polite to other sites.** It sends at most 3 requests at a time to any outside host, and it tries a light `HEAD` request before a full `GET`.

## The live view

While it runs, you see counters for pages, links, OK, broken, and blocked; a progress bar; the requests in flight; and the newest broken links as it finds them.

At the end, it prints a table of missing images and a table of broken links. Each row shows the pages that use the link, with the link text or image alt text, so you can find it fast.

## Reports

Each run saves three files to `reports/`:

| File | Use |
|---|---|
| `site-DATE.mdown` | A checklist grouped by the page you need to edit. Each fix is a `- [ ]` checkbox. |
| `site-DATE.csv` | One row per broken link per page. Sort and filter in a spreadsheet. |
| `site-DATE.json` | The same data for scripts. |

The checklist looks like this:

```markdown
### [My Trip to Japan](https://example.com/japan/)
`/japan/` · 2 to fix

- [ ] **404** 🖼 Missing image: [\[alt: Mount Fuji\]](https://example.com/wp-content/uploads/fuji.jpg)
- [ ] **404** 🔗 Broken link: [Tokyo guide](https://oldsite.example/tokyo)

## Links that use an IP address

- [ ] **200** 🖼 image: `http://203.0.113.7/wp-content/uploads/map.png`
    - change to: `https://example.com/wp-content/uploads/map.png`
```

To use it in [Bear](https://bear.app), drag the `.mdown` file into Bear or use File › Import. The note gets the `#linkchecker` tag.

## Recommendations

Many broken links share one cause: an old address. That might be the IP address of a server you moved away from, or a domain you no longer use. One search and replace can fix all of them at once.

After the crawl, linkchecker:

1. Groups the broken links and IP-address links by their start, for example `http://203.0.113.7/`.
2. Swaps that start for your site's address in a few sample URLs (5 by default).
3. Requests each new URL to see if it works. A URL that only redirects to your home page does not count.
4. Recommends the swap if the samples work.

| Mark | Meaning |
|---|---|
| ✅ | Every sample worked. Safe to run. |
| ⚠️ | Some samples worked. Run it, then upload the files that are still missing. |

Groups where no sample works get no recommendation. That is usually a dead outside site.

The terminal shows a RECOMMENDATIONS table. The `.mdown` checklist has a RECOMMENDATIONS section near the top. For each fix it shows the search and replace strings, the sample results, a WP-CLI command, and checkboxes for the dry run and the real run.

> ⚠️ **Back up your database before you run any search and replace.** It changes every matching row at once, and you cannot undo it.

## Fixing IP-address links in WordPress

IP-address links usually come from a server move. Posts written on the old server kept its address in their links and image URLs. When the old server shuts down, those images and links break.

You can fix all of them at once with the free [Better Search Replace](https://wordpress.org/plugins/better-search-replace/) plugin:

1. **Back up your database first.** A search-and-replace changes every matching row, and you can't undo it.
2. In WordPress, go to Tools › Better Search Replace.
3. In **Search for**, type the old address, for example `http://203.0.113.7`. In **Replace with**, type your site address, for example `https://example.com`.
4. Select the tables to change. `wp_posts` holds post content. `wp_postmeta` holds some image data.
5. Leave **Run as dry run** turned on and run it. Check how many rows it would change.
6. If the numbers look right, turn off dry run and run it again.
7. Repeat for each IP address in the report. If any links use `https://` with the IP, search for that form too.

Then run linkchecker again. The IP-address section should be empty. The fix works only if the files also exist on your current server. Any image that wasn't copied over during the move will now show up as a missing image, and you'll need to upload it again.

If you use [WP-CLI](https://wp-cli.org/), `wp search-replace 'http://203.0.113.7' 'https://example.com' --dry-run` does the same job from the command line.

## Options

| Option | What it does |
|---|---|
| `-c, --concurrency N` | Requests at once (default 12) |
| `-m, --max-pages N` | Stop after N pages. Good for a quick trial run. |
| `-t, --timeout SECS` | Timeout for each request (default 20) |
| `--no-external` | Check only links on your own site |
| `--no-sitemap` | Don't read the sitemap; only follow links |
| `-x, --exclude REGEX` | Skip URLs that match. You can repeat it. |
| `--no-recommend` | Skip testing search-and-replace fixes |
| `--fix-samples N` | Sample URLs to test for each fix (default 5) |
| `--show-blocked` | Also list blocked and timed-out links in the terminal |
| `-o, --out DIR` | Folder for reports (default `reports`) |
| `--no-report` | Don't save report files |

Some examples:

```zsh
# Quick trial on the first 50 pages
uv run linkchecker.py https://example.com --max-pages 50

# Only your own site, and skip tag pages
uv run linkchecker.py https://example.com --no-external -x '/tag/'
```

Press **Ctrl-C** to stop early. You still get the results so far and the report files.

The tool exits with code `1` when it finds broken links and `0` when it finds none, so you can use it in a script or a scheduled job.

## Requirements

- Python 3.10 or later
- [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/) (recommended). It reads the dependencies from the top of the script.

Without uv:

```zsh
pip install httpx rich
python3 linkchecker.py https://example.com
```

## Limits

- It reads the HTML the server sends. It does not run JavaScript, so it misses links that only scripts add.
- It skips some WordPress URLs that are not real content: `/wp-admin`, `/wp-login.php`, `/feed`, `/wp-json`, `?replytocom=`, and `?share=`.
- A timeout on another site counts as "check by hand," not "broken," because slow sites are common.

